Usage:
  rhythm-soundcheck snapshot <url> --eaa     the baseline, with a draft
  rhythm-soundcheck compare --eaa            what changed since
  rhythm-soundcheck statement -o accessibility-statement.md

EAA mode gathers accessibility evidence for the European Accessibility Act.
It runs the health, a11y and keyboard modules in Chrome with the WCAG 2.1 A
and AA rules (--wcag 2.2 for the newer ones). A list from --modules or the
config file keeps its modules and gains a11y and keyboard. Every run writes
a statement draft (eaa/statement-draft.md) and an evidence pack
(eaa/evidence.json) into its folder.

What the tests do:
  - a11y: axe-core 4.14.0 on every page with the rules of the WCAG version,
    plus fields labeled only by a placeholder, sideways scrolling at 320 px
    wide and known overlay widgets.
  - keyboard: Tab and Shift+Tab through every page; menus, dialogs and
    disclosures opened and closed by keyboard; your key flows at --level
    flows (help keyboard).
  - statement: the draft lists what the tests found per criterion, with
    pages and counts (help statement); evidence verify and sign check the
    pack (help evidence).

What they can't do:
Of the 50 WCAG 2.1 A and AA success criteria, the tests look for failures of
27 (29 of the 55 in WCAG 2.2). The other 23 need a person. Even for a
criterion the tests look at, only a person can say it is met. A person's
review can follow WCAG-EM, the W3C's method for evaluating a website:
https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/conformance/wcag-em/

Why the draft has [TO DO]s:
Only you can say who runs the site, how visitors reach you, which status
fits, what is out of scope and what a person checked. The draft marks each
of these [TO DO]. Put your details under statement: in
.soundcheck/config.yaml (rhythm-soundcheck init --eaa writes the block) and
run rhythm-soundcheck statement again: your text lives there, so a new draft
never loses it.

Limits:
  - Automated and keyboard tests find some accessibility problems, not all.
    This is evidence for your own review, not a statement that the site
    meets WCAG, EN 301 549 or the European Accessibility Act.
  - Soundcheck's EAA mode gives you evidence about your site. It is not an
    audit, not a certification and not legal advice.
  - Many success criteria need a person: meaningful alt text, captions,
    reading order, plain language. The draft marks them not tested: needs a
    person, never passed.
  - Only the pages and flows Soundcheck visited, in the states it saw, in
    Chrome, with the keyboard walk at desktop width. Content in iframes from
    other sites, PDFs, videos, apps and emails was not tested.
  - The keyboard walkthrough uses Chrome's keyboard events. It is not a
    screen reader test, not a voice control test and not a test with
    disabled users.
  - The site decides what Soundcheck sees. A site that serves headless
    browsers a different page can pass while visitors fail. Overlay widgets
    don't make a site accessible.
  - EAA mode presses keys on your pages. It holds requests that would change
    data, blocks downloads and follows no links except skip links. A site
    that changes data on a GET or on focus can still change. Run it on
    staging and list your live site under production.
  - Reports contain what your pages showed. Soundcheck masks form values,
    credentials and tokens it recognises and emails, phone numbers and card
    numbers in text it writes. Masking is pattern-based: it is
    pseudonymised, not anonymised. Screenshots are not masked.
  - A signed evidence pack proves who produced it and that it wasn't changed
    afterwards. It doesn't prove the site was in that state for every
    visitor.
  - Nothing leaves your machine except requests to your own site and hosts
    you allow. No telemetry, no hosted service, no AI.
